<?xml version='1.0' standalone='yes'?>
<!DOCTYPE PLUGIN [
<!ENTITY name        "backblaze64-monitor-beta">
<!ENTITY author      "rogman">
<!ENTITY version     "2026.08.15-beta20">
<!ENTITY launch      "Utilities/backblaze64-monitor-beta">
<!ENTITY github      "rogleete/backblaze64-unraid-dash-monitor-beta">
<!ENTITY pluginURL   "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/&github;/main/backblaze64-monitor-beta.plg">
<!ENTITY packageVER  "&version;">
<!ENTITY packageName "&name;-&packageVER;">
<!ENTITY packageFile "&packageName;.txz">
<!ENTITY packageURL  "https://github.com/&github;/releases/download/&version;/&packageFile;">
<!ENTITY pluginLOC   "/boot/config/plugins/&name;">
<!ENTITY emhttpLOC   "/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;">
]>

<!--
Backblaze64 Monitor (BETA): an Unraid dashboard tile for Backblaze64
(iamfoz/backblaze-64-personal-wine-container). This beta build talks to
the container's key-authenticated /api/v1/status endpoint instead of
scraping the built-in web monitor, so it works whether the container's
own Web Authentication is on or off.

Once this is working as intended it will be folded back into the
stable repo (rogleete/backblaze64-unraid-dash-monitor); until then it's
tracked separately here so beta testing doesn't touch anyone on the
stable release.

Requires a Backblaze64 container reachable from this Unraid server, and
an API key generated from that container's own Web UI (API tab, Read
permission). Configure address, port, and the key under
Settings -> Utilities -> Backblaze64 Monitor Beta after installing.
-->

<PLUGIN  name="&name;"
         Title="Backblaze64 Monitor Beta"
         author="&author;"
         version="&version;"
         launch="&launch;"
         pluginURL="&pluginURL;"
         icon="cloud-upload"
         min="6.9.0"
         project="https://github.com/&github;"
         readme="https://github.com/&github;#readme"
>

<DESCRIPTION>
BETA build: dashboard tile for Backblaze64, now backed by its key-authenticated /api/v1/status API instead of the web monitor page. Shows live upload speed, backup progress, file counts, failures, memory usage, and connection latency on your Unraid Dashboard, with a drag-and-drop settings page to choose which fields appear and where.
</DESCRIPTION>

<CHANGES>
### &version;
- Found the actual root cause of the Current File spacing on a real
  Dashboard tile, confirmed via getBoundingClientRect() measurements:
  a 4px margin-top (confirmed applied via getComputedStyle) was
  producing an ~18px real gap -- 14px more than intended, and not
  reproducible in the Settings page's Live Preview at all despite
  identical CSS and identical PHP-generated HTML. That pointed at
  align-content redistributing space between wrapped flex lines when
  the container has more cross-axis room than its content needs, which
  a real dashboard tile's height constraints can trigger and a preview
  box never does -- no margin value could have fixed that, since it
  isn't a margin problem. Restructured Current File to render as its
  own sibling block after the Scan Progress/Backup Total row instead of
  a third item wrapped inside that row's flex container, so its
  spacing is a plain, deterministic block margin that can't be
  redistributed by any flex ancestor's line-spacing behavior

### 2026.08.15-beta19
- Fixed the real causes of the Current File spacing/alignment issues,
  found after comparing the Dashboard tile against the Settings live
  preview (which looked correct while the Dashboard tile didn't -- that
  mismatch was the key clue):
  - The Dashboard tile's stylesheet is loaded through a second, separate
    URL that Unraid's own register_dashboard_tile() uses (independent
    of the versioned stylesheet link already in the tile's own markup),
    and that second URL had no cache-busting version string. Every CSS
    fix since beta15 was reaching the Settings page immediately but
    likely sitting stale on the Dashboard tab until a hard refresh --
    now versioned the same way
  - The progress bar wasn't vertically centered against the filename
    text (align-items:flex-start pinned it to the top of the line
    instead of matching the centered alignment every other row2 item
    uses), which was the actual source of the line looking uneven --
    not the margin value. Switched to align-items:center to match
    Scanning/Backup, and reverted the margin back to 4px (pixel-
    verified equal to the row1->row2 gap) since beta18's 2px was
    compensating for the alignment bug rather than a real gap problem

### 2026.08.15-beta18
- Tightened the gap above the Current File line from 4px to 2px.
  Measured via getBoundingClientRect() in beta17: at 4px it was
  pixel-identical to the row1->row2 gap, but still visually read as
  larger -- the wider bar and long filename there carry more visual
  weight than the compact "Backup: 96%" line above, so an equal gap
  reads as bigger by contrast. 2px compensates for that

### 2026.08.15-beta17
- Current File now only shows while the API reports an actual active-
  transfer phase (Uploading or Finishing, the two values confirmed
  against real API responses) rather than just checking that a
  filename is present. Some APIs leave the last-known filename in
  place even while idle or paused and only change the phase to say so
  -- if that's the case here, checking file alone could have kept
  showing a stale filename forever instead of hiding, the way Scan
  Progress correctly hides when no scan is running

### 2026.08.15-beta16
- Fixed the Current File line's spacing again -- splitting row-gap
  from column-gap in beta15 wasn't reliable (flex "gap" support varies
  across browser versions, and the result was actually worse for some).
  Replaced it with a plain, explicit margin-top instead, set to match
  the exact same ~4px rhythm already used between the collapsed summary
  line and the row below it, so the spacing is deterministic rather
  than depending on flexbox gap behavior at all

### 2026.08.15-beta15
- Fixed excess vertical spacing before the Current File line -- it
  looked like two blank lines between "Scanning/Backup" and the
  filename below. The row2 container used a single "gap" value that
  applied to both the horizontal spacing between items on the same
  line and the vertical spacing between wrapped lines; split those
  apart (row-gap vs column-gap) so wrapping onto its own line stays
  tight instead of inheriting the wider horizontal spacing value

### 2026.08.15-beta14
- Fixed Current File showing nothing even with a file actively
  transferring and read:files enabled: the field mapping in beta13 was
  a guess at an API shape that turned out not to exist. Checked against
  a real API response, the filename actually lives at
  activity.file, and there's no ready-made per-file percentage (the
  in_flight[] entries are per-upload-thread, not per-file, since a
  single large file uploads via many parallel part-uploads) -- the
  correct percentage comes from files.chunk_map, which names the same
  file as activity.file and lists every chunk sent so far out of its
  total. Verified against two real API responses with known chunk
  counts (39/53 and 17/53 sent) and confirmed the computed percentage
  matches exactly

### 2026.08.15-beta13
- Added a "Current File" checkbox on the Item Sorting tab, below Scan
  Progress / Backup Total. When checked, shows the file currently being
  backed up on its own line below those two -- a small progress bar
  (same size as Scan Progress's) first, then the filename, no "Current
  File:" label. Long filenames wrap to additional lines rather than
  overflowing or truncating. The filename text uses the exact same
  font-size and styling as "Scanning:"/"Backup:" (reuses the same CSS
  rule) for visual consistency
- Requires the API key on the Connection tab to have read:files
  permission, not just the default read scope -- noted directly next to
  the new checkbox. Without it, checking the box shows nothing rather
  than an error, the same graceful no-op Scan Progress already uses
  when no scan is running

### 2026.08.15-beta12
- Added a fourth settings tab, "Style Theme": pick how the tile's
  expanded field list is laid out, independent of Color Theme (which
  only changes colors). Default plus 7 new layouts -- Rings (circular
  gauges for anything with a percentage), Compact (dense single-column
  list with a dotted leader line), Cards (bordered stat cards with a
  thin accent bar), Segmented (a retro block/segment meter instead of a
  smooth bar), Spotlight (Upload Rate as a big hero stat, everything
  else in a tight grid below), Minimal (pure typography, no boxes or
  bars at all), and Icons (icon + label/value rows with a thin inline
  bar). Each of the 8 cells previews the style's own real renderer fed
  sample data, not a mockup, arranged 2 columns x 4 rows with Default
  first. Whichever Color Theme is selected still applies on top of
  whichever style is chosen -- the two are independent settings
- Because Style Theme changes actual HTML structure rather than a CSS
  variable, its live preview goes through the same server round trip
  Item Sorting already uses (Color Theme stays instant/client-side,
  since only its colors need to change)

### 2026.08.15-beta11
- Fixed the real cause of the blank/white Dashboard when both this
  beta and the stable plugin are installed together: both defined an
  identical set of global PHP function names (bbmon_normalize(),
  bbmon_fetch(), bbmon_render_body(), etc. -- the historical beta1
  changelog note about a "plugin-specific prefix" turned out to
  predate a later fork, so the prefix wasn't actually unique between
  the two). Unraid loads every enabled dashboard tile's PHP into one
  shared request to build the page, so having both plugins define the
  same function names threw an uncaught "Cannot redeclare function"
  fatal error partway through that build -- with error display off,
  nothing was printed and nothing reached the webserver's own log, so
  the page just came back empty below the nav bar with a normal-
  looking 200 response. Every one of this plugin's global functions is
  now prefixed bbmonbeta_ instead of bbmon_, verified by loading both
  plugins' PHP in a single request and confirming no redeclaration
  occurs regardless of what the stable plugin's own internals look
  like

### 2026.08.15-beta10
- Updated the default field selection and order on a fresh install (or
  after "Reset to Defaults") to match a real-world tuned layout: State
  moved out of the collapsed line's neighboring Column 2 slot and out
  of Column 2 entirely (off by default), Last Backup/Total Files/
  Completed Files/Failed Files/Health/First Backup switched on by
  default, Uploads Today stays off, and both Scan Progress and Backup
  Total are on by default in the second collapsed row. Column 1/Column
  2 order updated to match

### 2026.08.15-beta9
- Color Theme tab: replaced the two sample-value demo rows (Memory,
  Backup Total) with a single full-width legend bar per theme, hard-
  stopped at the real color thresholds (bad under 34%, elevated 34-66%,
  good from 67% up) so all three of a theme's colors show at once, at
  the percentages where they'd actually apply -- rather than one
  arbitrary sample percentage per field, which could make a theme look
  "reversed" depending on which side of a threshold the demo numbers
  happened to land on
- Every theme's "elevated" color is now a distinct tint of that theme's
  own hue instead of a shared amber -- previously every theme's warn
  color clustered in the same narrow amber range, so a field like
  Backup Total sitting in the 34-66% band looked "orange-ish" under
  every theme even though good/bad did visibly change

### 2026.08.15-beta8
- Renamed the plugin's internal identifier from "backblaze64-monitor" to
  "backblaze64-monitor-beta" (the Title already said "(Beta)", but the
  actual install path, config file, and dashboard-tile registration
  were still identical to the stable plugin's, so installing this
  overwrote/collided with a stable install instead of running
  alongside it). Now installs to its own
  /boot/config/plugins/backblaze64-monitor-beta and
  /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/backblaze64-monitor-beta, with its own
  settings page route (Settings -> Utilities -> Backblaze64 Monitor
  Beta) and its own dashboard-tile registration, config file, and
  in-page element IDs/JS state -- so both can be installed and shown on
  the Dashboard at the same time without either one clobbering the
  other's files or state
- If you'd previously installed a build under the old
  "backblaze64-monitor" identifier, remove it first (Plugins ->
  Backblaze64 Monitor (Beta) -> Remove) before installing this one, so
  its now-orphaned files under the old path don't linger

### 2026.08.15-beta7
- Renamed the settings tab from "Themes" to "Color Theme"
- Fixed theme selection needing an unrelated tab-switch-and-toggle
  before it would highlight or enable Apply -- cells now use a real
  radio input under the hood (still click-anywhere-on-the-cell to
  select), so the browser's own change event does the work instead of
  a custom click handler that wasn't wiring up reliably
- Every theme now recolors the full good/elevated/problem color
  progression, not just the "good" accent -- previously only fields
  using the "high is bad" scale (like Memory) picked up theme colors at
  their lower/no-status range, while "high is good" fields (like Backup
  Total) stayed on Unraid's stock orange once they crossed into the
  cautionary range, regardless of theme. Fixes the Backup Total bar
  showing plain orange at ~60% progress under every theme
- Added 6 more themes (Teal, Coral, Indigo, Lime, Steel, Plum) for 12
  total, and moved the settings grid to 3 columns to fit them

### 2026.08.15-beta6
- Removed the Pause / Backup Now button added in beta3/beta4 -- it
  wasn't completing requests reliably (control.php returning a 200
  with an empty body rather than the expected JSON), and rather than
  ship that half-working, this reverts back to beta2's plugin with only
  the scan-progress documentation note kept. The Connection tab's API
  Key description and the post-install message are back to beta2's
  wording too, since neither the button nor the Control-permission note
  that went with it apply any more. May revisit once the underlying
  issue is tracked down
- Kept: the Item Sorting tab now notes that Scan Progress (both in
  Expanded Columns and the second collapsed row) only appears on the
  tile while a scan is actually running
- Also cleaned up a stray, incorrectly-named empty directory
  (`{css,js,include}`) that had been shipping inside the package since
  an early build

### 2026.08.15-beta4
- A rejected Pause/Backup Now click (wrong permission, nothing running
  to pause, client tool unavailable, etc.) now shows the API's own
  reason as a small red line under the tile, instead of the button just
  flashing "Error" with the actual cause only visible in the browser
  console

### 2026.08.15-beta3
- Added a Pause / Backup Now button to the collapsed tile's second row
  (Item Sorting tab -- "Start/Stop Backup", off by default). Reads
  "Pause" in light orange while a backup is running, "Backup Now" in
  light green while paused or stopped; always sits at the far right of
  that row regardless of what else is showing on it. Needs Control
  permission on the API key (Connection tab now notes this) -- a key
  without it still shows the button, and the rejected click's own error
  shows in its place rather than hiding the button up front
- Item Sorting tab: noted that Scan Progress (both here and in Expanded
  Columns) only appears on the tile while a scan is actually running

### 2026.08.15-beta2
- Added four more API-backed fields, off by default: Health (reads
  "OK" in green, or the first reported issue in red), Uploads Today
  (success/failure counts for the day), First Backup (only appears
  while a first pass is still running, in "Day X" form -- gone
  entirely once it finishes), and Scan Progress (only appears while a
  file-list scan is running, with a progress bar -- gone entirely
  otherwise)
- Added a second, fixed-order row to the collapsed tile summary --
  Scan Progress and Backup Total as compact progress bars. Unlike the
  row above, these two don't drag-reorder, only show/hide; the row is
  left out of the tile entirely when neither has anything to show

### 2026.08.15-beta1
- Switched the data source from scraping the container's built-in
  /monitor/ page to its new key-authenticated /api/v1/status endpoint.
  Works whether the container's own Web Authentication is on or off,
  and no longer depends on that page's internal markup
- Connection tab: replaced Secure Connection with an API Key field
  (generate one from Backblaze64's own Web UI -> API tab -> Read
  permission). Scheme (http/https) is now detected automatically
  instead of asked for
- Removed the Data Points tab -- each field on the Item Sorting tab's
  Expanded Columns now carries its own show/hide checkbox instead,
  alongside the existing drag-to-reorder handle
- Added four new fields, sourced from the API and off by default:
  Last Backup (days since), Total Files, Completed Files, and Failed
  Files (shown in red once non-zero; sourced from the API's
  skipped_files count -- files the client has given up on entirely)
- Added Failed Files as an option for the tile's collapsed summary line
- Tile now reads "Please add API key to plugin settings" when no key
  is configured, and "Error with API access, check plugin settings and
  key access" if the API can't be reached or the key is rejected

### 2026.08.14 (carried over from the stable plugin this beta forked from)
- Fixed the live preview being unreadable (dark card background with
  black text, and a white-on-white "Live Preview" label)

### 2026.08.13
- Added a "Collapsed Tile Data" section on the Item Sorting tab
- "Backup Complete" in green instead of "0 files left" at 0 remaining
- Collapsed-line ETA reads "ETA 2d 3h" instead of "Backup ETA 2d 3h"
- Added the live preview above the settings tabs

### 2026.08.12
- Renamed shared PHP functions to a plugin-specific prefix so this
  plugin and the original Backblaze64 Dash Monitor plugin can run
  side by side
- Hardened the tile against unexpected/missing fields in a response

### 2026.08.11
- Initial release
</CHANGES>

<!--
===========================================
PRE-INSTALL
===========================================
-->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
# Remove old package versions, keep only the one being installed
rm -f $(ls &pluginLOC;/&name;*.txz 2>/dev/null | grep -v '&packageVER;')
mkdir -p &pluginLOC;
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<!--
===========================================
PACKAGE INSTALLATION
===========================================
-->
<FILE Name="&pluginLOC;/&packageFile;" Run="upgradepkg --install-new">
<URL>&packageURL;</URL>
<SHA256>239ef60a0aa8599c4cb6b54ec8195972258e10ae4bdc64e1961242ad1ca6b549</SHA256>
</FILE>

<!--
===========================================
POST-INSTALL
===========================================
-->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
echo ""
echo "----------------------------------------------------"
echo " Backblaze64 Monitor (BETA) has been installed."
echo " Version: &version;"
echo ""
echo " Next step: go to Settings -> Utilities -> Backblaze64 Monitor Beta"
echo " and enter your Backblaze64 container's address and port, plus"
echo " an API key generated from its own Web UI (API tab, Read"
echo " permission), then check the Dashboard page."
echo "----------------------------------------------------"
echo ""
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<!--
===========================================
UPDATE
===========================================
-->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="update">
<INLINE>
rm -rf &emhttpLOC;
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<!--
===========================================
REMOVE
===========================================
-->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove">
<INLINE>
removepkg &packageName; 2>/dev/null

# Keep the user's saved settings (server address, key, toggles) on the
# flash drive in case they reinstall later. Uncomment to wipe them too:
# rm -rf &pluginLOC;

echo ""
echo "----------------------------------------------------"
echo " Backblaze64 Monitor (BETA) has been removed."
echo " Your settings were kept at &pluginLOC; in case you"
echo " reinstall -- delete that folder manually if you"
echo " don't want them kept."
echo "----------------------------------------------------"
echo ""
</INLINE>
</FILE>

</PLUGIN>
